#: processes/kg/mount-subgraph.bpmn:57
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "`declaredSubgraph(start, id)`: the declaring layer, the entry, and the resolved source with `declaredIn` saying which layer answered. Never read `source` off the declaration directly — the config override and the legacy `storage` field are folded in by the resolver and nowhere else. A contradiction (both `source` and `storage`; a `qa` subgraph keyed by tip) throws here, before anything is mounted."
msgstr ""

#: processes/kg/mount-subgraph.bpmn:82
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "A `branch` source keyed by `tip`: `branch-store mount --id <dir-id>` writes the tip's files at the declared path, so every reader finds the directory where it always was; `push` splices edits back onto the tip, never forcing, and a same-file race is a conflict. The branch's NAME is the declaring directory's `storage` (or `source`) — a branch no declaration names is refused rather than guessed. A `commit`-keyed branch is read per commit through its own store (`qa-store`), not mounted."
msgstr ""

#: processes/kg/mount-subgraph.bpmn:73
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "A `directory` source IS its declared path. Nothing is mounted; a write is an ordinary commit on the working branch, which is why `branch-store push` answers \"commit through git\" for it rather than pushing anything."
msgstr ""

#: processes/kg/mount-subgraph.bpmn:95
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "A `remote` source: another repository's tree at a full 40-character commit. `bun run cat mount:remote` lays it down at the declared path and locks its tree digest; it is never written back, because the bytes are somebody else's at somebody else's pin. Usually reached through a `source.remote` layer in the root `index.config.json` (legacy: `remoteMounts`), which expands a whole harness and its closure — `Process_MountDependency`."
msgstr ""

#: processes/kg/mount-subgraph.bpmn:117
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "A kind added to the union (a graph database, say) before this diagram and the mount tool learn it. Refused with its own exit code — reading it as a directory would scan an empty path and report it clean."
msgstr ""

